1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:12,224
I am the registrar. I work for the centre's administrator, such as a research director, and keep one list of every lab, core facility and collaboration, and who leads each.

2
00:00:12,599 --> 00:00:23,498
I keep one list of the centre's labs, cores and collaborations, each with its lead and its status, and I hold to one rule: each PI leads at most one lab or core.

3
00:00:23,498 --> 00:00:37,344
I show the administrator which members, in any lab, have expired or missing training, such as research ethics. And I sign each PI's identity card, the signed record that says who they are.

4
00:00:37,749 --> 00:00:50,320
I see each lab from the outside. Each lab keeps its own notebooks, code, memory and member list, and edits them itself. Archiving a lab changes its status in the list, and its files stay.

5
00:00:50,320 --> 00:00:57,386
When a collaboration needs channels and folders, I approve them and the centre millwright sets them up.

6
00:00:57,799 --> 00:01:03,126
A real run that only reads. The administrator asks me for the centre-wide view.

7
00:01:03,126 --> 00:01:14,760
I find one lab and its PI, no cores or collaborations yet, and one gap named plainly: nobody is recorded as registrar. I changed nothing.

8
00:01:15,150 --> 00:01:28,463
To use me, the administrator types to Claude Code and names me: ask the registrar which labs and cores are registered, or whether an entry is incomplete. I refuse a PI who already leads another group.

9
00:01:28,463 --> 00:01:45,299
When I record, find a conflict or skip, the first line says so: Recorded, Conflict or Skipped. A centre chooses its own registrar and records its own labs, cores and collaborations.
